<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    JWT/JWS verification profile "orders-api", compact serialization
    (header.payload.signature). Runs when signature.verify.type = "jwt"
    AND signature.verify.profile = "orders-api" AND
    signature.verify.jwt.detached != "true" (see the -Detached sibling
    policy for that case).

    Native VerifyJWS — no custom crypto needed, unlike Cavage/XML-DSig.
    continueOnError defaults to false, so an invalid/expired/malformed
    JWS raises a policy fault here and the request is rejected; nothing
    else needs to check a "valid" flag. Add a FaultRule if you want a
    response shape other than Apigee's default JWS-fault response.

    KeyStore/Alias name the cert in Apigee's KeyStore holding the
    orders-api signer's public key — set up separately from this repo
    (Apigee KeyStores aren't managed as bundle config). Algorithm is
    pinned here deliberately rather than sourced from KVM: letting
    KVM (or worse, the token itself) choose the accepted algorithm
    would open an algorithm-confusion downgrade attack.

    ASSUMPTION TO VERIFY: I can't test this policy against a live
    Apigee instance from here. <KeyStore>/<Alias> as plain literal
    values (no `ref` attribute) is the documented static form; if your
    Apigee version needs a different PublicKey shape, adjust this
    policy accordingly — the rest of the shared flow doesn't depend on
    its internals.

    To add another profile: copy this policy (and its -Detached
    sibling) with a new profile suffix, new KeyStore/Alias/Algorithm,
    add Steps for it in default.xml gated on
    signature.verify.profile = "<new profile>", and add that profile
    to RF-Unsupported-Jwt-Verify-Profile's Condition.
-->
<VerifyJWS name="VerifyJWS-Jwt-orders-api-Compact" continueOnError="false">
    <DisplayName>VerifyJWS-Jwt-orders-api-Compact</DisplayName>
    <Algorithm>RS256</Algorithm>
    <Source>signature.verify.jwt.jws</Source>
    <PublicKey>
        <KeyStore>orders-api-keystore</KeyStore>
        <Alias>orders-api-signing-cert</Alias>
    </PublicKey>
    <VariablePrefix>signature.verify.jwt</VariablePrefix>
</VerifyJWS>
